<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koine Greek</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as koine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Koine Greek was the common form of Greek that spread across the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> after the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. It served as the language of administration, commerce and culture in the Hellenistic kingdoms, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>. Its spread was one of the most lasting legacies of Seleucid rule, and it later became the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koine-greek.jpg" alt="Koine Greek" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greek_Script_from_Rosetta_Stone_(36140196440).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Attic dialect became the basis of the <b>koine</b>, the common Greek of the Hellenistic world and of the New Testament.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek language, art and philosophy spread across Egypt, Mesopotamia, Persia and Central Asia, influencing government, literature and science for centuries, and <b>Koine Greek</b> became a common language of administration and commerce.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…most transformative and enduring contribution to world history was the dissemination of Hellenistic Greek culture, its language, urban planning, philosophy, art and science, across the arc of territory from the Mediterranean coast to the borders of India, creating a cultural <b>koine</b> that shaped every civilisation within its reach.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A common form of the language, the <b>Koine</b>, based on Attic, replaced the old dialects and was spoken as a first or second language by millions of people of many origins.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The result was the Hellenistic world: a vast zone in which a common form of Greek, the <b>koine</b>, became the language of government, commerce, and learning.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander's conquests made Greek the language of government, trade and culture from Egypt to Afghanistan, and the common <b>Koine</b> replaced the old dialects.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li></ul></section>
